WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.281
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Tuesday, November 17.

2
00:00:09.281 --> 00:00:22.514
Three things today: Trump floats a Kim Jong Un meeting at APEC, North Korea's Jo Yong Won pledges support to Putin in Moscow, and Lee Jae-myung and Japan's Takaichi hold their fourth summit in Manila.

3
00:00:22.514 --> 00:00:33.365
The APEC summit opens tomorrow in Shenzhen, and Kim Jong Un's attendance, which would trigger both a Trump meeting and a Putin meeting, remains unconfirmed tonight.

4
00:00:33.365 --> 00:00:42.430
Education Minister Choi Kyo-jin is expected to announce specific anti-cheating measures today, two days before the college entrance exam.

5
00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:45.170
First, the APEC wildcard.

6
00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:58.535
Donald Trump told reporters at the White House before leaving for the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation summit in Shenzhen that he may meet Kim Jong Un, saying Kim's attendance was, in his words, "up to President Xi."

7
00:00:58.535 --> 00:01:03.955
The Kremlin added its own signal, saying Putin would meet Kim "of course" if he attends.

8
00:01:03.955 --> 00:01:15.288
That's two of the world's most consequential leaders publicly welcoming a Kim appearance, before Pyongyang has said a word about it. South Korea's Foreign Ministry responded carefully.

9
00:01:15.288 --> 00:01:25.758
A ministry official said Seoul and Washington are in close communication on the possibility, and that Seoul "supports dialogue between the United States and North Korea."

10
00:01:25.758 --> 00:01:39.616
But analysts quoted in the Korea Times say there are no signs of the diplomatic groundwork you'd expect before a summit, and they warn that a deal struck without South Korean input could leave Seoul facing new security risks.

11
00:01:39.616 --> 00:01:51.072
North Korea has stated in recent weeks that recognition of its nuclear status is a precondition for any engagement. Meanwhile, North Korea content has vanished from Chinese social media.

12
00:01:51.072 --> 00:02:01.050
Posts on Weibo, Rednote, and WeChat have been disappearing since at least late October, with searches for "Kim Jong Un" and "Pyongyang" returning no results. N.K.

13
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:08.318
News attributes that crackdown to heightened bilateral sensitivity and possible preparations for a Kim visit to China.

14
00:02:08.318 --> 00:02:20.759
Kim himself spent November 15 and 16 inspecting the new Mount Phothae ski resort near the Chinese border, calling tourism a "major stimulant to regional development." KCNA published over 70 photographs.

15
00:02:20.759 --> 00:02:30.860
The border location, the Chinese social media blackout, and the Trump and Putin remarks are all pointing in the same direction, but Pyongyang has confirmed nothing.

16
00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:41.023
Second, Jo Yong Won in Moscow. While Trump was floating a summit, North Korea's number-two party figure was already meeting Russia's president.

17
00:02:41.023 --> 00:02:51.302
Jo Yong Won, a Presidium member of the Political Bureau and secretary of the Workers' Party of Korea Central Committee, met Vladimir Putin at the Kremlin on Monday.

18
00:02:51.302 --> 00:03:04.590
Jo vowed to support Moscow's struggle against what he called U.S. "neocolonialism and neo-Nazism." He also met the speaker of Russia's State Duma and addressed a general meeting in Russia. The timing is striking.

19
00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:10.545
Those talks took place just days before the planned Trump-Xi-Putin meeting at APEC in Shenzhen.

20
00:03:10.545 --> 00:03:20.072
Jo's visit is a public show of alignment, North Korea signaling solidarity with Russia at precisely the moment the major powers are converging in China.

21
00:03:20.072 --> 00:03:30.853
The Kremlin's statement that Putin would meet Kim "of course" at APEC came the same day, suggesting the Moscow talks and the APEC signals are part of a coordinated message.

22
00:03:30.853 --> 00:03:45.708
The RAND National Defense Research Institute published analysis this week arguing that closer ties among China, Russia, North Korea, and Iran have made the existing U.S.-South Korea combined deterrence posture strategically insufficient.

23
00:03:45.708 --> 00:03:56.050
That report calls for both countries to reassign roles and responsibilities in response. The Jo-Putin meeting is exactly the kind of event that analysis had in mind.

24
00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:06.490
Third, Lee and Takaichi in Manila. South Korea and Japan held their fourth bilateral summit since Japanese Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi took office.

25
00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:13.994
President Lee Jae-myung and Takaichi met on the sidelines of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations summit in Manila.

26
00:04:13.994 --> 00:04:24.351
Lee said South Korea-Japan relations have "brightened more than ever," pointing to record-high favorable public sentiment between the two countries in a recent survey.

27
00:04:24.351 --> 00:04:37.064
He confirmed he will visit Japan in January as invited. The two leaders agreed to strengthen energy and economic security cooperation given Middle East developments, and to deepen strategic communications.

28
00:04:37.064 --> 00:04:44.630
Lee posted afterward that "continued shuttle diplomacy confirms that trust between our two countries is growing stronger."

29
00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:54.180
That is the fourth bilateral summit in roughly a year, a pace that reflects how much the relationship has shifted. The January visit will be one to watch.

30
00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:56.414
Now to Pyongyang.

31
00:04:56.414 --> 00:05:12.779
State media's headline story was Kim Jong Un's inspection of the Mount Phothae ski resort near the Chinese border on November 15 and 16, a domestic welfare and development story, though the location has drawn outside attention given the speculation about a China visit.

32
00:05:12.779 --> 00:05:25.738
The other notable item from outside North Korea: Daily N.K. reports that Chinese used vehicles have been entering North Korea through Hyesan customs since mid-October, ending a prolonged freeze on vehicle imports.

33
00:05:25.738 --> 00:05:29.084
Jo Yong Won's Moscow visit dominated external coverage.

34
00:05:29.084 --> 00:05:40.460
The bottom line from state media: Pyongyang led with domestic tourism and welfare content at home, while projecting strategic alignment with Russia to the outside world, days before APEC.

35
00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:50.222
The rest of the news, quickly. A Russian military aircraft entered South Korea's air defense identification zone without notice early Tuesday.

36
00:05:50.222 --> 00:05:58.483
South Korea's Joint Chiefs confirmed the incursion; fighters were deployed and the aircraft exited without entering territorial airspace.

37
00:05:58.483 --> 00:06:10.964
South Korea signed the STARLIFT space launch cooperation memorandum of understanding at NATO headquarters in Brussels on October 29, becoming the only non-NATO founding member of the multinational framework.

38
00:06:10.964 --> 00:06:22.481
The Defense Ministry says the arrangement will diversify military space access and create export opportunities for Korean launch and satellite firms. Deputy Foreign Minister Kim Jina met U.S.

39
00:06:22.481 --> 00:06:34.962
Under Secretary of State for Economic Affairs Jacob Helberg in San Francisco on Monday to discuss artificial intelligence cooperation and implementation of the 350 billion dollar strategic investment pledge.

40
00:06:34.962 --> 00:06:37.917
On the economy, Seoul is approaching a milestone.

41
00:06:37.917 --> 00:06:53.775
Industry Minister Kim Jeong-gwan visited Incheon port this week marking the moment South Korea approaches one trillion dollars in annual exports, which would make it only the fourth country to reach that threshold, alongside Germany, the United States, and China.

42
00:06:53.775 --> 00:06:58.840
The minister called for identifying new export growth drivers beyond semiconductors.

43
00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:14.939
The minister also convened a critical minerals public-private strategy meeting Tuesday and announced the launch of an interagency Resource Security Council and a new industry resource security division, citing inadequacy of past overseas resource development efforts.

44
00:07:14.939 --> 00:07:24.225
South Korea was elected to the International Telecommunication Union Council for the tenth consecutive time at the ITU Plenipotentiary Conference in Doha.

45
00:07:24.225 --> 00:07:35.621
Park Jong-min was separately elected to the ITU Radio Regulations Board. The National Assembly Budget and Accounts Special Committee began substantive review of the fiscal year 2027 budget.

46
00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:45.510
Ten standing committees have collectively proposed a net increase of approximately 25 trillion won, including an 8 trillion won increase from the Welfare Committee.

47
00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:52.203
In a Gallup Korea poll taken November 3 to 5, President Lee Jae-myung's approval rose two points to 41 percent.

48
00:07:52.203 --> 00:07:59.740
The Democratic Party edged up to 36 percent, while the People Power Party fell to 28 percent, level with unaffiliated voters.

49
00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:13.613
Japan denied entry to Cho Kuk at Haneda Airport. Japanese authorities cited unspecified grounds under immigration law; Cho's party called it unprecedented for a public figure attending an academic event.

50
00:08:13.613 --> 00:08:27.735
Seoul expressed regret and requested an explanation from Tokyo. Korea joined the Carbon Market Coalition at a high-level climate meeting in Antalya on November 16, becoming the 13th member alongside the EU and China.

51
00:08:27.735 --> 00:08:41.466
Climate Minister Kim Sunghwan announced the membership. Two days before the CSAT exam, Prime Minister Han Seong-sook chaired an interagency meeting and announced an emergency task force on AI-assisted cheating.

52
00:08:41.466 --> 00:08:53.954
Han said at a public address: "Smart devices will be completely banned from CSAT exam rooms; glasses and watches will be inspected in full; buttons on clothing will be covered with stickers."

53
00:08:53.954 --> 00:09:01.800
President Lee chaired a video meeting on CSAT security from Manila the same evening, ordering a zero-tolerance response.

54
00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.109
Beyond the region.

55
00:09:04.109 --> 00:09:16.549
Seoul will provide 2 million dollars through UNICEF to pre-position relief supplies in Peru ahead of forecast El Nino flooding, in line with United Nations anticipatory action funding allocated in July.

56
00:09:16.549 --> 00:09:28.436
In Europe, Czech nuclear regulators began their first on-site quality assurance inspection of the Dukovany nuclear plant project, where Korea Hydro and Nuclear Power is the selected contractor.

57
00:09:28.436 --> 00:09:33.240
South Korea's National Assembly is separately weighing a hearing on that deal.

58
00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:49.938
Finally, the markets. Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, fell about 2.6 percent Tuesday to close at about 4,968, breaking below 5,000 for the first time since February. The trigger, according to SBS, was new U.S.

59
00:09:49.938 --> 00:10:05.582
restrictions on artificial intelligence semiconductor exports to China, which hit Samsung Electronics and SK Hynix hard. The dollar bought about 1,336 won, up slightly. Brent crude was just under 93 dollars a barrel.

60
00:10:05.582 --> 00:10:14.200
That's the Korea Daily Brief for Tuesday, November 17. The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
